readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|''Ascaunce'' that craft is so lyght to lere?
{{right|''Chaucer'', Yeoman’s Tale, l. 838.}}}}
'''II.''' ''conj.'' As if; as if (saying).
{{smaller block|And wroot the names . . .
''Ascaunce'' [var. ''askaunce''] that he wolde for hem preye.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Summoner’s Tale, l. 37.}}}}
{{smaller block|Keeping a countenance ''ascances'' she understood him not. {{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Therewith he raysed his heavy head alight,
''Askaunces'', Ha! indeed and thinkest thou so.</poem>
{{right|''Gascoigne'', Flowers. (''N. E. D.'')}}}}

'''askant''' (a̤-skant′), ''adv.'' [Early mod. E. also ''ascant, askaunt, ascaunt'', later form of ''askance''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''aslant'' or ''asquint''.] Sidewise; askance.
{{smaller block|With an eye ''askant''. {{right|''Cowper'', Iliad, xi. 657.}}}}

'''asker<sup>1</sup>''' (ȧs′kėr), ''n.'' [ME. ''asker, askere''; < ''ask''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who asks; a petitioner; an inquirer.
{{smaller block|To give to every ''asker''. {{right|''Hammond'', Works, I. 99.}}}}
{{smaller block|Every ''asker'' being satisfied.
{{right|''Sir K. Digby'', The Nature of Bodies.}}}}

'''asker<sup>2</sup>''' (as′kėr), ''n.'' [E. dial. also ''ascar, askard, ascard, askerd, askal'', etc.: see ''ask''<sup>2</sup>.] Same as ''ask''<sup>2</sup>. [Prov. Eng.]

'''askew''' (a̤-skū′), ''adv.'' [Early mod. E. also ''askue, ascue, a skew''; appar. < ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''skew'', q. v. Cf. equiv. Icel. ''ā skā''.] In an oblique position; obliquely; awry; out of the proper position or arrangement; hence, askance; sidelong.

readings.c3s1:
{{smaller block|The shaft drove through his neck ''aslant''. {{right|''Dryden.''}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>As with his wings ''aslant''
Sails the fierce cormorant.</poem>
{{right|''Longfellow'', Skeleton in Armor.}}}}
'''II.''' ''prep.'' Slantingly across; athwart.
{{smaller block|There is a willow grows ''aslant'' a brook.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iv. 7.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The swelling upland where the side-long sun
''Aslant'' the wooded grove at evening goes.</poem>
{{right|''Longfellow'', Spirit of Poetry.}}}}

'''asleep''' (a̤-slēp′), ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [Also ''on sleep'' (Acts xiii. 36); ME. ''aslepe'', ''aslape'', ''onslæpe'', etc., < AS. ''on slæpe'', in sleep; < ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''sleep''.] '''1.''' In or into a state of sleep: as, to fall ''asleep''.
{{smaller block|He [Sisera] was fast ''asleep''. {{right|Judges iv. 21.}}}}
{{smaller block|By whispering winds soon lull’d ''asleep''.
{{right|''Milton'', L’Allegro, l. 116.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And there within the hollow lay . . .
Aslaug the golden-headed child,
''Asleep'' and rosy.</poem>
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, III. 32.}}}}
'''2.''' Figuratively—(''a'') Dead; in or into a state of death: chiefly in the Scriptures and religious literature.
{{smaller block|Concerning them which are ''asleep'', . . . sorrow not.
{{right|1 Thes. iv. 13.}}}}
(''b'') Dormant; inactive; idle.
{{smaller block|<poem>During this inquisition Julia’s tongue
Was not ''asleep''.</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Don Juan, i. 145.}}}}
'''3.''' Having a peculiar numb feeling, accompanied by or passing off with a prickly tingling sensation. {{smaller|This condition is produced usually by prolonged pressure on the nerve-trunks, and consequently is most frequent in the arms and legs.}}

readings.c3s2:
¶
{{smaller block|His legge . . . was all ''aslepe'', and in a manner sterke stiff.
{{right|''Udall'', tr. of Erasmus’s Apophthegms, p. 235.}}}}
'''4.''' ''Naut.'', said of sails when the wind is just strong enough to distend them and prevent them from shaking.

'''aslope''' (a-slōp′), ''pp.'', or ''prep. phr.'' as ''adv.'' or ''a.'' [< late ME. ''a slope'', either < ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''slope'', ''n.'', or else for ''aslope'', ''aslopen'', ‘slipped away,’ < AS. ''āslopen'', pp. of ''āslūpan'', slip away, < ''ā-'' + ''slūpan'', slip: see ''a-''<sup>1</sup> and ''slope'', ''a.'' and ''n.'', and ''slip''. Cf. ''alight''<sup>1</sup>, of similar double formation.] In or into an inclined or slanting position or direction; with leaning or inclination; deflected from the perpendicular; with declivity or descent, as a hill.
{{smaller block|Set them not upright, but ''aslope''.
{{right|''Bacon'', Essays.}}}}

'''aslug†''' (a-slug′), ''adv.'' [< ''a''<sup>3</sup> + ''slug''<sup>1</sup>.] In a sluggish manner. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>His boat
That comes ''aslug'' against the stream.</poem>
{{right|''Fotherby'', Atheomastix, ii. 12.}}}}

'''-asm.''' [< Gr. -ασμός, < -άζειν, after -ι-, equiv. to -ισμός, < -ίζειν: see ''-ism'', and cf. ''-ast''.] A suffix of Greek origin, occurring instead of ''-ism'' after ''-i-'', as in ''enthusiasm'', ''miasm'', etc.

'''asmanite''' (as′man-īt), ''n.'' A form of silica found in some meteorites. {{smaller|It has been supposed to be orthorhombic in crystallization, but is probably identical with tridymite.}}

